Falsifies `system_tests::user::riscv_virtio_tests::the_page_tables_say_u_mode_cannot_read_the_kernels_memory`.

Architecture: riscv64

The defect is `user_can_read` no longer reading the `U` bit, and on this ISA that is the
property rather than a proxy for it. The test's own doc comment says so: aarch64 has
`AT S1E0R` and RISC-V has no such instruction, so `mmu::user_can_read` walks the installed
tables in software and reads `U`, "and that software answer is the thing under test". This
patch makes it answer "yes, if it is mapped at all", which is the confused deputy the test is
named after.

**This patch did not turn the test red the first time it was run, and that is the most
important line in this file.** Measured 2026-09-16, `script/falsifications --sweep kernel`:
SURVIVOR. The reason was not a weak defect. `user_can_read` walked through
`translate_user`, and `translate_at` builds its `Mapper` with `Half::Low`, always, so a
high-half address comes back `None` before any leaf is read. The assertion this test is named
for could not fail: it answered "no, U-mode cannot read the kernel" by refusing to look, and
had done since milestone 41.

**The tree had already written down the cause and nothing connected it.**
`is_mapped_in_current_space`, forty lines away, exists for precisely this and says why in its
own doc comment: *"a user thread reaching for the kernel's memory names a high-half address ...
`translate_user` alone would say 'not mapped' for it and turn the most interesting case into
the wrong answer."* `user_can_read` went on calling `translate_user`.

Milestone 305 fixed that (`translate_in_either_half`) and this patch is against the fixed
function, so the record below is of a test that now means what it says.

Expected to fail: `assert!(!mmu::user_can_read(kernel_va), "the page tables say U-mode could
read the kernel's own memory")`.

**Why the defect is here and not in the mapping.** The aarch64 siblings of this record map the
kernel's own memory user-readable, one flag at one call site, and the test then watches the
hardware refuse. **That defect cannot be booted on RISC-V at all.** `crates/paging`'s Sv39
encoder turns `CAP_USER` into the `U` bit, and S-mode access to a `U` page raises a page fault
unless `sstatus.SUM` is set, which this kernel sets only inside a test helper. So a kernel that
marked its own `.rodata` user-readable would not be a kernel userspace can read; it would be a
kernel that cannot read itself, dead before the first test. RISC-V's one-root design is what
the test calls its single line of defence, and it is also what makes that defect
unrepresentable.

**The rubber-stamp guards stay green, which is what makes this a real falsification rather
than a broken function.** The test also requires `user_can_read(IMAGE_BASE)` (the process's own
text, mapped and `U`) and `!user_can_read(STACK.start)` (its own half, unmapped). Under this
patch the first is still true because the page is mapped, and the second is still false
because nothing is there. A checker that simply said yes to everything would fail the second,
and the red would be about the checker rather than about the kernel.

**`user_can_write` is left reading `U` on purpose.** The line below the one that fires,
`assert!(!mmu::user_can_write(kernel_va))`, stays green, so this record covers the read half
of row 21 on this ISA and not the write half.

diff --git a/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/mmu.rs b/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/mmu.rs
index 5d35dec63..40aa1724e 100644
--- a/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/mmu.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/arch/riscv64/mmu.rs
@@ -726,7 +726,7 @@ fn translate_in_either_half(va: u64) -> Option<(u64, Flags)> {
 /// (`the_page_tables_say_u_mode_cannot_read_the_kernels_memory`).
 #[cfg_attr(not(feature = "system_tests"), allow(dead_code))]
 pub fn user_can_read(va: u64) -> bool {
-    translate_in_either_half(va).is_some_and(|(_, f)| f.is_user_accessible())
+    translate_in_either_half(va).is_some()
 }
 
 /// Whether U-mode may write `va`: user-accessible and writable. Same disposition, same test.
